WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.530
- Autonomous driving is a
big technological challenge.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.550
Probably the biggest of our generation.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.800
- [Narrator] This is a Tesla.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.230
It comes with a feature
known as autopilot.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.290
And Elon Musk says in the future,

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.180
it'll have a feature called.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.013
- Full self-driving.

00:00:15.013 --> 00:00:16.500
- [Narrator] Several other
cars being sold today

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.140
also come with assisted driving technology

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.530
which still requires
someone behind the wheel.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.240
But new vehicles that won't
have anyone in the driver's seat

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.230
are getting closer to production.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.660
Startups Waymo, Cruise,
TuSimple, and Aurora,

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.530
are already testing driverless tech

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.670
on some public roads across America.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.600
- It's gonna move quickly.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.640
We'll start to see this
move from prototypes

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.470
to actually scaling products (indistinct).

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.430
- We see us becoming
fully driverless in 2024.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.280
- [Narrator] As they
invest billions in R&amp;D

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.480
and sign multi-billion dollar deals,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.410
their valuations have soared
and TuSimple has gone public.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.210
They're pitching a future where
people won't even own cars.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.480
- Younger generations won't
even think about owning a car.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.610
- [Narrator] And some
jobs will no longer exist.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.560
- We fundamentally believe
that every trucker will be able

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.970
to retire as a trucker.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:05.803
- [Narrator] But first of all,

00:01:05.803 --> 00:01:07.180
they need to convince governments

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.380
and the public that
their technology is safe.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.540
- You're limiting your potential

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.680
if consumers are not comfortable
getting into your vehicle

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.660
and taking a ride.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.160
- [Narrator] So could
these startups make driving

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.413
a thing of the past?

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:19.780
Tesla has been working

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.090
on making its vehicles
autonomous since around 2016

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.170
and said it planned to
have self-driving cars

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.610
on the road by 2020.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.040
- And sometimes I'm not on time.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.890
But I get it done.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.610
- [Narrator] For now,
experts say these cars

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.730
have what they call level two autonomy.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.770
- It is a level two system
where they're saying

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.610
that the driver has to be there,
they have to pay attention.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:40.920
They're counting on them.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:41.753
- [Narrator] What does that mean?

00:01:41.753 --> 00:01:43.690
Well, it's a scale used by auto engineers

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:44.910
and it starts at zero.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.110
- Which means, you know,
kind of a 1950s car

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.930
with no automation at all.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.530
- [Narrator] And goes up to level five.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.060
- [Tekedra] Basically when
the car can do everything,

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:55.893
anywhere.

00:01:55.893 --> 00:02:00.050
- You can drop it off in Afghanistan today

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.600
and it will be able to operate
without maps by itself.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.010
- [Narrator] Instead of releasing vehicles

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.710
and working towards autonomy,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.040
these top startups want
to roll them out directly

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.820
at level four autonomy.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.620
Which is where the car
doesn't need a driver

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.700
as long as it's preloaded with information

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.780
about its surroundings
like maps and directions.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.280
- And we just simply think
level four is the goal

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.400
for any domain.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.350
- [Narrator] Some companies
in the US and Asia

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.550
like Baidu AutoX and Didi,

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.840
say they've reached level
four in some vehicles

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.660
though none have rolled
them out at commercial scale

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:33.860
but some are getting close.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.770
Waymo and Cruise have
piloted a fleet of robotaxis

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.330
in some ring-fenced areas around the US.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.470
Think ride-hailing without the driver.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.190
Like this, the Waymo One.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.900
- We're the only company

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.410
that has a fully autonomous
ride-hailing service

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:51.190
available to the public today
in the Phoenix metro area.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:52.480
- [Narrator] In late 2020,

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.070
Waymo began removing support drivers

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.130
from its vehicles after its cars racked up

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.650
some 20 million miles on public roads,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.630
gathering troves of
data for its algorithms.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.920
- The car shows up completely empty.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.260
They take a ride from point A to point B.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.110
That's one of the ways you make this real,

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.310
is you launch a service in the city

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.080
and you see if customers will
actually adopt the service

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:13.913
and use it.

00:03:13.913 --> 00:03:16.820
- [Narrator] Waymo started
as a Google project in 2009,

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.520
and now has a $30 billion
valuation and Alphabet,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.540
Google's parent, as a
majority shareholder.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.940
Cruise has roughly the
same valuation as Waymo

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.430
and is owned by GM.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.400
With whom it has built an
autonomous vehicle from scratch.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.480
It has accrued fewer miles
than Waymo, some two million,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.480
in it's tests in San Francisco.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.920
- Miles driven absolutely matters

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.100
because ultimately the goal
is to put this technology

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.320
on public roads,

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.030
interacting with other
human-driven vehicles.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.520
So you do need that experience
at some point to say,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.680
yes, this is not only safe,
but it's actually viable.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.960
- [Narrator] Cruise
recently got permission

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.700
to remove the support driver

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.080
but it's yet to carry paying passengers.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.270
It plans to begin
production of this vehicle,

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.440
the Origin, in 2023.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.750
We spoke with company
representatives over the phone,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.170
who said GM's backing
is a strategic advantage

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.280
but it's not just cars,
startups are also eyeing trucks.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.703
- The trucking industry
is a massive industry.

00:04:16.591 --> 00:04:18.350
It's $4 trillion globally,

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.430
800 billion dollars in the US alone.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.790
- [Narrator] That's the market Aurora

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.660
and TuSimple are betting on.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.060
Aurora which has received
investment from Uber

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.160
says it has a handful of test vehicles out

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.160
on public roads in the US.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.010
TuSimple has around 60
autonomous trucks circulating

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.800
on highways in the Southwest
with support drivers.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.000
It was the first driverless
company in the US

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.433
to go public, getting a
roughly $8.5 billion valuation.

00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:43.266
- In the US alone, 60,000
driver shortages this year

00:04:45.544 --> 00:04:47.890
that will increase to
about 160,000 by 2028

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.640
based on latest numbers.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.940
So it's a very real problem.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.240
- We don't intend to ever
build a car or build a truck.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.130
We work with great companies
like Paccar and Volvo

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.950
and Toyota, and integrate our
technology to their vehicles.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.700
- I think consumer goods
can provide, you know,

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:04.690
an easier path.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.180
You're already kind of
simplifying the situations

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.270
that you would have to deal with.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.390
If you're driving the same pathway often,

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.950
you're not gonna have pedestrians
and cyclists, you know,

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.120
interacting with traffic.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:15.953
- [Narrator] But their tech needs

00:05:15.953 --> 00:05:17.490
to overcome some big challenges

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.390
before it can be widely adopted.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.750
- And the more we discover
about the challenges,

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.000
the more we learn about how
long this is gonna take.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.010
And so I think you saw all of industry

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.100
start to reset expectations.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.111
- [Narrator] Waymo and others
had previously said 2020

00:05:30.111 --> 00:05:33.270
was the year that robotaxis
would become commonplace

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.990
across the US but the coronavirus pandemic

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.250
and technical hurdles

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.140
have led to forecasts being pushed back.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.170
One issue, these vehicles
still don't work well at night

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.570
or in bad weather.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.350
- It's not a coincidence that
you see companies testing

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.520
in fair weather places first.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.690
- When you think about the barriers,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.360
it's really engineering barriers.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.540
It's time, it takes capital,
but we'll get there.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.960
- [Narrator] But those
are not the only barriers.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.220
- A Honda Civic right there
colliding with the Waymo van.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.350
We're being told it was not
at fault in this accident.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.890
- [Narrator] The industry has
to address safety concerns.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.750
As autonomous cars have been involved

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.170
in a number of accidents in recent years.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.340
After a fatal crash in 2018,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.420
where a car's built in automatic
emergency braking system

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:20.810
was disabled, Uber had to stop
testing autonomous vehicles

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:21.810
in Arizona.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.430
It continued tests in other cities

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.380
before selling its unprofitable
self-driving car unit

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.280
to Aurora at the end of 2020.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.850
All players in this industry know

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.170
that they have to deal
with a lot of skepticism.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.350
- There's this suggestion
that the technology

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:38.250
is presenting new risk or first risk.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.900
The reality is though roads have risk.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.410
- So it's not possible to
make anything 100% safe,

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.410
whether it's a toothbrush or an airliner.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.810
And so we try to make these systems

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.730
really safe.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:53.640
- [Narrator] A recent study found

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.080
that even if autonomous
vehicles cause accidents

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.240
at half the rate of human drivers,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.050
only 37% of Americans would opt in.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.060
- We've got more forgiveness,
we've got more understanding

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.350
if that happens or is caused
by another human driver.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.460
I don't think we have that
same compassion for a robot

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.500
that might produce the same result.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.070
- [Narrator] To show that
self-driving vehicles

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.980
because very few accidents,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:18.270
Waymo started disclosing
all of its crashes

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.880
and near misses in Arizona,

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:24.030
a total of 47 episodes over 2019 and 2020.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.510
- Because we thought
it was really important

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.140
to start to demonstrate

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:31.210
that you insert a fully autonomous
vehicle into an ecosystem

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.740
and there is still human
error around the vehicle.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.570
- Sure, we've got disengagements,
we've got miles driven,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.080
we've got the number of crashes

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.850
and we can relate that to human drivers,

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.380
but that doesn't necessarily
make it much easier

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.670
to kind of say, this is
safe, safer, or safe enough.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.050
So just being able to
quantify these things

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.561
I think it's still a
challenge for the industry.

00:07:53.561 --> 00:07:56.144
(gentle music)

